DONE-WHEN MET 2026-08-31: the owner received a real push notification on a real device, in the installed PWA of beta, and tapping it opened the app.

That is the only evidence that could close this task, and it resolves the ambiguity S2 left open. S2's probe returned statusCode 201 with nothing rendering, and a push service returns 201 WITHOUT decrypting — so a payload encrypted against the wrong p256dh/auth is indistinguishable from success on the server side. A notification rendering is what disambiguates it.

What this confirms end to end, none of which any test could:
  - the base64url round-trip of p256dh/auth through savePushSubscription (Task 12's encoding);
  - VAPID signing and AES128GCM encryption inside the 'use node' action on beta;
  - the push service accepting AND the browser successfully DECRYPTING the payload;
  - the service worker's push handler rendering it (Task 13);
  - notificationclick focusing/opening the app through the origin clamp.

The rest of this task's acceptance was already unit-tested: two subscriptions both receive, a dead endpoint is removed on 404/410, removing an absent endpoint does not throw. Four gates green.

Code shipped in 5bef2a0 -> afaf57f -> 50b6698 -> 79bda50. Review found one Critical: the raw WebPushError was passed to console.error and carries the push endpoint as an own enumerable property, so every 401/403/413/429/5xx would have written a capability URL into the Convex logs — with the comment directly above claiming the endpoint was not logged. Fixed via convex/lib/pushErrors.ts. Two owner-approved divergences also landed: removal scoped to the owning player, and https:-only endpoint validation.

notes:
STATUS 2026-08-27: the verifier is confirmed LIVE on the deployment - a bogus signature returns 403 'Invalid signature' from https://fabulous-goldfish-949.convex.site/polar/webhook. That proves standardwebhooks is running and rejecting.

WHAT REMAINS UNPROVEN IS EXACTLY ONE THING: that Polar's OWN bytes verify. The key derivation is proven byte-identical to the SDK's (sign under the SDK-derived key, verify under the shipped key, across 8 secrets including non-ASCII, CJK and emoji), and convex/http.test.ts drives the whole protocol with REAL Standard Webhooks signatures. So the mechanism is sound; what no test here can produce is a genuine Polar delivery.

THE FIRST REAL DELIVERY IS THE TEST. If it 403s, the secret registered in Polar does not match POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET on the deployment - check that before suspecting the verifier, since a mismatched secret and a broken verifier are indistinguishable from the status code alone.

WATCH FOR: a 403 on every delivery means the secret is wrong or empty (an empty key is accepted by standardwebhooks and would 403 everything - http.ts:120-124 guards construction against that, so an empty secret gives 500, not 403). A 202 on every delivery means signatures verify but identity does not resolve - that is decision F territory, not this issue.
THE UNPROVEN HALF IS NOW PROVEN, 2026-09-03, by Phase 5's sandbox pass.

This issue's notes said: "WHAT REMAINS UNPROVEN IS EXACTLY ONE THING: that Polar's OWN bytes verify... THE FIRST REAL DELIVERY IS THE TEST."

Real Polar deliveries have now been through the whole path on beta:
  - a checkout completed and subscription.active upgraded the correct account, so the signature verified AND identity resolved off the snake_case wire shape;
  - that same delivery, REDELIVERED from the Polar dashboard, returned 200 and changed nothing;
  - subscription.canceled arrived and correctly did nothing;
  - subscription.revoked downgraded the account and applied the team limit.

So neither of the failure modes this issue warned to watch for occurred: no blanket 403 (the registered secret matches POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET) and no blanket 202 (identity resolves).

THE DOCUMENTATION HALF IS DONE IN THE SAME COMMIT:
  - the spec's webhook-pipeline block no longer shows validateEvent; it shows the standardwebhooks construction and the JSON.parse of the wire shape;
  - decision F now carries the wire-shape amendment, since F is the identity decision and reading external_id vs externalId is exactly what changed under it;
  - the spec's "Divergences from v1" section records it, marked explicitly as a divergence from the PLAN rather than from production, so it earns no 7a row - v1 was on Lemon Squeezy and shares none of this path;
  - the plan's Task 10 carries a superseded-block in the same style as its ACKNOWLEDGED_EVENTS correction, naming the import and call to ignore and spelling out the knock-on into Task 5.

Both halves of the DONE-WHEN are therefore satisfied.

description:
Reported on beta: the scores table border looks full white and does not match other components.

ROOT CAUSE, and it is not the table. Tailwind v4 changed the DEFAULT border colour from gray-200 to currentColor. v2/src/styles.css's @layer base block sets box-sizing and body styles but never sets a default border colour, so every bare `border` utility inherits the text colour — near-white on a dark background, near-black on light.

Affected today (bare `border`, no colour class): ui/card.tsx, ui/dialog.tsx, ui/select.tsx (content), ui/badge.tsx, ui/popover.tsx, ui/dropdown-menu.tsx (x2), components/scores-table.tsx.

Not affected: anything passing a colour explicitly — ui/input.tsx and ui/select.tsx's trigger use border-input, board-entry/form.tsx uses border-input, ThemeToggle uses border-line-subtle. That mismatch is exactly why the table reads as "not matching other components": some components name a colour and some rely on a default that no longer exists.

FIX: add the standard shadcn Tailwind-v4 base rule to styles.css so a bare `border` resolves to the design token:

  @layer base {
    * { border-color: var(--color-border); }
  }

One rule fixes the table and every Card, Dialog, Popover, Dropdown and Badge at once. --color-border already maps to --border, which is --border-subtle (#e4e4e7 light / #292929 dark).

Also reported: the border looks DOUBLED between rows. Re-check after the colour fix — a 1px currentColor border is far heavier than the token and may account for the perception. If it survives, suspect the sticky pinned cells interacting with the table's border-collapse.

Found by the owner on beta after the Phase 2 auto-deploy.
